- How would you adapt this for multi-producer/multi-consumer safely?
- What tests would you run on a simulated interrupt workload to prove correctness?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design crash‑consistent data paths in persistent memory, a common production issue when power failures occur during in‑flight operations.

## Key Concepts

- Durability guarantees for PMEM writes
- Memory ordering: release/acquire, fences, cacheline flushes
- Recovery via redo log and slot sequence counters
- ABA avoidance in a concurrent ring
- Testing with fault injection and crash replay

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-code: enqueue/dequeue with PMEM flush/fence
// Slot: { data, seq, valid }
void enqueue(T v){
  size_t t = tail.fetch_add(1);
  Slot *s = &ring[t % CAP];
  s->data = v; // write data
  clwb(&s->data, sizeof(T));
  fence(); // ensure data persists
  s->seq = t; // publish with durable tail update (release)
  clwb(&s->seq, sizeof(size_t));
  fence();
  // publish tail already happens via atomic tail update
}

bool dequeue(T *out){
  size_t h0 = head.load();
  Slot *s = &ring[h0 % CAP];
  if (s->seq != h0) return false;
  *out = s->data;
  s->valid = false;
  clwb(&s->valid, sizeof(bool));
  fence();
  head.fetch_add(1);
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle power loss during the publish vs data write steps?
- How would you validate recovery correctness across multiple restart scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a simple, predictable memory allocator suitable for tight RTOS constraints, with deterministic O(1) operations and minimal fragmentation.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool design
- Free-list management via indices
- Interrupt-safe critical sections
- Pointer arithmetic and alignment
- Stress-testing under ISR vs task context

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define BLOCK_COUNT 512
static uint8_t pool_mem[BLOCK_SIZE*BLOCK_COUNT];
static uint16_t next_index[BLOCK_COUNT];
static uint16_t free_head;

void pool_init(void) {
  for (uint16_t i = 0; i < BLOCK_COUNT-1; ++i) next_index[i] = i+1;
  next_index[BLOCK_COUNT-1] = 0xFFFF;
  free_head = 0;
}

void* pool_alloc(void) {
  if (free_head == 0xFFFF) return NULL;
  uint16_t idx = free_head;
  free_head = next_index[idx];
  return pool_mem + idx * BLOCK_SIZE;
}

void pool_free(void* ptr) {
  uintptr_t off = (uintptr_t)ptr - (uintptr_t)pool_mem;
  uint16_t idx = off / BLOCK_SIZE;
  next_index[idx] = free_head;
  free_head = idx;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to multiple block sizes?
- How would you detect and recover from pool exhaustion in real-time constraints?

